# Cloudflare Pages cache-control config.
#
# Deliberately conservative: scripts/build.js does not content-hash
# CSS/JS/asset filenames (they're always /css/brand.css, /js/site.js,
# etc.), so an aggressive `immutable, max-age=1y` here would mean a
# browser that cached a file before a deploy keeps serving the OLD
# version for up to a year after the real file changes — a
# correctness bug, not just a missed optimization. These durations are
# short enough that a stale asset self-heals within the hour/day
# rather than requiring a hard-refresh, and `must-revalidate` means an
# expired-but-unchanged file still costs only a cheap conditional
# request (304), not a full re-download. Move to long-lived immutable
# caching only once/if the build gains content-hashed filenames.

/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate

/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/robots.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/sitemap.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY HEADERS
#
# Added after an audit found the site sending none at all. Each of the
# headers below is safe to apply site-wide — none of them can break a
# page that currently works.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, AND WHY:
#
#   Content-Security-Policy. A script-src policy is the header worth
#   having and it is the one that can silently break sign-in. Clerk
#   loads its SDK from a Frontend API host derived at runtime from the
#   publishable key (js/clerk-loader.js), so the correct allowlist
#   depends on which Clerk instance is configured, and Google Fonts adds
#   two more origins. A wrong CSP does not fail a build or a test — it
#   fails one real student trying to sign in, silently, in production.
#
#   It is not omitted because it does not matter. It is omitted because
#   shipping it honestly requires one thing this environment cannot do:
#   loading the deployed site and completing a real sign-in with the CSP
#   active. That is a ten-minute check against the live domain, and it
#   should be done before a CSP is added, not after.
#
# Permissions-Policy grants microphone to this origin only, because the
# Listening Lab records the learner — removing it would break the single
# most distinctive part of the programme. Camera and geolocation are
# denied outright: nothing here uses either, and a permission nothing
# uses should not be available to ask for.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: microphone=(self), camera=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  # CSP, in report-only. See the note below.
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; media-src 'self' blob:; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://*.clerk.accounts.dev https://*.clerk.com; connect-src 'self' https://*.clerk.accounts.dev https://*.clerk.com; worker-src 'self' blob:

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY REPORT-ONLY, AND WHY IT IS HERE AT ALL
#
# The note above explains why no ENFORCING Content-Security-Policy is
# set: Clerk's SDK loads from a Frontend API host derived at runtime
# from the publishable key, so the correct script-src depends on which
# Clerk instance is configured, and a wrong CSP does not fail a build or
# a test — it fails one real learner trying to sign in, silently, in
# production. That reasoning is sound and it still holds.
#
# It does not apply to report-only. `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`
# cannot break a page by construction: the browser evaluates the policy,
# reports what would have been blocked, and blocks nothing. So the
# argument against shipping an enforcing policy without a live sign-in
# check is not an argument against shipping this one — and without it,
# any successful injection anywhere on the site executes with full
# privileges, on a site that takes free-text input.
#
# The policy above is the intended ENFORCING policy, minus the things
# only a live check can confirm. What it needs before the -Report-Only
# suffix comes off:
#
#   1. Complete a real sign-in with this header live and confirm no
#      Clerk origin is reported as blocked. The wildcards cover the
#      documented hosts; a custom Frontend API domain (a College
#      subdomain rather than *.clerk.accounts.dev) would not be.
#   2. Remove 'unsafe-inline' from script-src. Several pages carry
#      inline JSON-LD and small bootstraps; each needs a nonce or a
#      hash first, and doing that badly is how a CSP breaks a page.
#   3. Add report-to/report-uri once there is somewhere to send the
#      reports. Without it the browser reports to the console only,
#      which is enough for a developer with the page open and nothing
#      at all for a learner in Lagos.
#
# Written down here rather than in an issue, because the last thing
# omitted with a good reason was CLERK_JWKS_URL, and it stayed omitted
# until an applicant found it.
